Example use:

@-moz-pref(slimline.feature.enabled) {
  .blah: margin-left: 2px;
}

.blah: margin-left: 6px;

Alternatives:
Currently it seems that the best option is to have JS reflect pref state in a parent node, and have 'preffed out CSS' include the reflected pref state in some way.

Pros:
The option above (along the lines of @-moz-document) could make it cleaner to develop features that are preffed out.

Cons:
Is this too much of a point solution - one that only applies to a limitted set of cases?

standard:
Here is a standardized description based on the provided enhancement request:

**Request:** Introduce Support for Pref-Conditional CSS Rules

**Objective:** Enable conditionally applying UA CSS style sheet rules based on boolean preferences, enhancing flexibility in feature development.

**Description:**

The enhancement aims to allow developers to include CSS rules that are dependent on specific user preference states. This can be achieved by introducing a syntax similar to `@-moz-pref`, which would enable conditional styling based on pref-enabled features. For instance:

```css
@-moz-pref(slimline.feature.enabled) {
  .blah { margin-left: 2px; }
}

.blah { margin-left: 6px; }
```

**Rationale:** This feature would provide a cleaner and more efficient way to develop preffed-out features by allowing conditional CSS application, similar to the existing `@-moz-document` syntax. However, it may be considered a point solution with limited applicability.

I've rephrased the original content in a clear, concise, and professional tone, removing direct references to specific wording from the original description.

description:
After ISO 8601 string parsing has been moved from ISO8601DateUtils.jsm to Date.parse (bug 543535 and bug 430930), there is no native facility to parse datetime strings with a local time offset attached, for example "2011-10-04T17:52:00+0200"

Firefox 7 parses successfully with
Components.utils.import("resource://gre/modules/ISO8601DateUtils.jsm");
alert(ISO8601DateUtils.parse("2011-10-04T17:52:00+0200"));

while
alert(Date.parse("2011-10-04T17:52:00+0200"));
returns NaN.

