standard:
Here is a standardized description of the enhancement request:

**Request:** Extend Text-Overshoot Handling to Support Mid-Crop Truncation with Preserved Character Blocks

**Description:** The existing CSS text-overflow property restricts string truncation to either the beginning or end of the string. This enhancement proposal introduces a new approach where strings can be truncated in the middle, preserving essential character blocks for improved readability.

**Key Features:**

1. **Middle Truncation**: Enable strings to be clipped at a specified midpoint, preserving context and reducing truncation artifacts.
2. **Character Block Preservation**: Allow specifying the number of characters on either side of the clipping point to prevent from being truncated, ensuring essential information remains visible.
3. **Customizable Overlap Handling**: Provide options for overlapping character block preservation on both sides of the clipping point.

**Proposed CSS Syntax:**

```css
/* Example 1: Mid-crop truncation with preserved right-side characters */
p {
  white-space: nowrap;
  width: 100%;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: clip ellipsis clip <number> [middle];
}

/* Example 2: Advanced character block preservation and overlap handling */
p {
  white-space: nowrap;
  width: 100%;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow-left: clip;
  text-overflow-middle: ellipsis;
  text-overflow-right: clip;
  text-overflow-left-preserve: <number>;
  text-overflow-right-preserve: <number>;
}
```

This rewritten description clearly conveys the main purpose of the enhancement request, details its key features, and provides a concise example of the proposed CSS syntax.

description:
Created attachment 616960
patch v1

We have lots of code that reads preferences, needs their live values and uses preference observers to achieve this (to not always read the value). That's a lot of boilerplate code to add every time if you don't want to add observers before the preference is accessed for the first time, etc.

We should have XPCOMUtils.defineLivePreferenceGetter() that eases the task:

XPCOMUtils.defineLivePreferenceGetter(this, "_myFeatureEnabled",
  "browser.myfeature.enabled");

So you can always access this._myFeatureEnabled and it will return the actual up-to-date value of the given preference. Also, a pref observer will not be added until we read the preference for the first time. The value won't be read before that, too.

A fourth parameter (aObserver) comes in handy if our code wants to be notified of preference value changes, too:

let Module = {
  init: function () {
    XPCOMUtils.defineLivePreferenceGetter(this, "_myFeatureEnabled",
      "browser.myfeature.enabled", this);
  },

  observe: function (aSubject, aTopic, aData) {
    if (this._myFeatureEnabled)
      dump("Whee, someone enabled me!");
  }
};

Not sure if we really need the fourth parameter but there are places (e.g. session store) that would need some kind of notification. We could also send a custom aTopic and make aData contain the new preference value.

layout/generic/nsSelection.cpp is full of hideous code like this:

  nsCOMPtr<nsIPresShell> shell;
  rv = GetPresShell(getter_AddRefs(shell));
  if (NS_FAILED(rv) || !shell) {
    return NS_ERROR_FAILURE;
  }

Currently we have

  #if defined(NS_STATIC_CHECKING) && defined(__cplusplus)
  inline int NS_FAILED(nsresult _nsresult) {
    return   _nsresult & 0x80000000;
  }

  inline int NS_SUCCEEDED(nsresult _nsresult) {
    return !(_nsresult & 0x80000000);
  }
  #else
  #define NS_FAILED(_nsresult)    (NS_UNLIKELY((_nsresult) & 0x80000000))
  #define NS_SUCCEEDED(_nsresult) (NS_LIKELY(!((_nsresult) & 0x80000000)))
  #endif

This was courtesy of bug 420933.  I'd like to remove the "defined(NS_STATIC_CHECKING) &&" part, so this is done for all C++ code.  Otherwise, macro expansions can be hard to read, like this assert from bug 767169:

  Assertion failure: (((__builtin_expect(!!(!((nsRange::CompareNodeToRange(lastCandidate, mRange, &nodeBefore, &nodeAfter)) & 0x80000000)), 1)))), at content/base/src/nsContentIterator.cpp:1295

This should read:

  Assertion failure: (NS_SUCCEEDED(nsRange::CompareNodeToRange(lastCandidate, mRange, &nodeBefore, &nodeAfter)), at content/base/src/nsContentIterator.cpp:1295

Should I keep the #if for __cplusplus, or can C handle inline functions on all the compilers we care about?

From nsEditor::DoTransaction:

    // We start off with an EditTxn since that's what the factory returns.
    nsRefPtr<EditTxn> editTxn = new PlaceholderTxn();

    // Then we QI to an nsIAbsorbingTransaction to get at placeholder
    // functionality
    nsCOMPtr<nsIAbsorbingTransaction> plcTxn;
    editTxn->QueryInterface(NS_GET_IID(nsIAbsorbingTransaction),
                            getter_AddRefs(plcTxn));
    // have to use line above instead of "plcTxn = do_QueryInterface(editTxn);"
    // due to our broken interface model for transactions.

    // save off weak reference to placeholder txn
    mPlaceHolderTxn = do_GetWeakReference(plcTxn);

editTxn is not used thereafter, only plcTxn.  This is outdated -- bug 489851 got rid of the factory functions that the comments refer to, so we could just use nsRefPtr<PlaceholderTxn> here, which requires no QI to be an nsIAbsorbingTransaction.  But then do_GetWeakReference doesn't work.  What's the right way to work around this?

Currently, it returns an nsresult, but all implementations return NS_OK. Instead, the boolean should be returned directly; the signature should be

virtual bool IsEnabled() const;

http://mxr.mozilla.org/mozilla-central/ident?i=IsEnabled&tree=mozilla-central&filter=&strict=1

The type errors here are ludicrous.  I'm seeing things like

  bool F();
  nsresult G()
  {
    return F();
  }

where bools are silently converted to nsresults or vice versa.  Or things defined to return pointers return NS_OK, which silently converts to a null pointer (!!).  An enum would fix this.

