In js/src/ion/Lowering.cpp, the optimization to form LCompareDAndBranch nodes instead of doing a separate comparison and branch checks for a compareType() of Compare_Double. This means it misses cases that use Compare_DoubleMaybeCoerceLHS and Compare_DoubleMaybeCoerceRHS. This causes it to sometimes generate code like this:

  ucomisd %xmm1, %xmm0
  seta    %cl
  movzbl  %ecx, %ecx
  [...]
  testl   %ecx, %ecx
  jne     [...]

for Javascript code like this (warning, contrived testcase):

var array = [ 0.1, undefined ];

for (var i = 0; i != array.length; ++i)
  if (array[i] < 0)
    print('hi');

x86's ucomisd can perform comparisons like DoubleLessThan with NaN properly, so there's no need for a separate NaN check for Javascript code like this (warning, contrived testcase):

var array = [ 0.1, undefined ];

for (var i = 0; i != array.length; ++i)
  if (array[i] < 0)
    print('hi');

standard:
Here is a standardized description of the enhancement request:

**Enhancement Request**

**Title:** Simplify Floating-Point Comparisons on x86 by Eliminating Redundant NaN Checks

**Description:**
Optimize common floating-point comparisons on x86 architectures by leveraging the native comparison functionality of `ucomisd`. This modification will eliminate unnecessary NaN (Not a Number) checks, improving performance and code efficiency.

Specifically, this enhancement will enable accurate comparisons involving NaN values without requiring additional checks, as demonstrated in the following example:
```javascript
var array = [ 0.1, undefined ];

for (var i = 0; i != array.length; ++i)
  if (array[i] < 0)
    print('hi');
```
By implementing this change, the code will correctly handle NaN values during comparisons, reducing redundancy and enhancing overall performance.

