or something like.

Currently, in order to invisibly parse an HTML page into a DOM document object, you have to:

1. download the content of the page (via f.e. XMLHttpRequest);
2. stick the content of the page into a data: URL;
3. create a hidden iframe (perhaps with JavaScript and other features disabled);
4. create a channel from the data: URL with the LOAD_BACKGROUND flag set;
5. call nsIURILoader::OpenURI, passing it the channel and the iframe's docShell.

Following this procedure prevents accidental display of the "download" dialog box (if your HTML page turns out to have a content type you weren't expecting), may have negligible impact on UI responsiveness (still in the process of testing this), and may prevent the cursor from changing into the busy cursor (still testing this, too).  But it's a painful way to obtain these benefits.

There should be an better way to do this.

Say I want to switch to tab "FooBar", but this tab does not appear in the AwesomeBar because either:
- I have actually closed that tab, I just do not remember it; or
- the tab is still open, but there many pages titled "FooBar" and the AwesomeBar only displays many entries and not tab "FooBar".

In either case, I have the following scenario:
1. type "FooBar" in the AwesomeBar;
2. search unsuccessfully for the opportunity to switch to that tab;
3. close the AwesomeBar to avoid losing my current window;
4. open a new tab;
5. re-type "FooBar";
6. either select or complete the address and press return.

What I would like is the following scenario if the tab is open:
1. type "FooBar";
2. "switch to tab" appears at the top of the list;
3. selecting it switches to tab.

The following scenario if the tab was recently closed:
1. type "FooBar";
2. "reopen tab" appears at the top of the list;
3. selecting it reopens a tab.

And the following scenario if the tab is closed:
1. type "FooBar";
2. either shift-select or complete the address and press shift-return;
3. doing this opens the address a new tab.

